<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Java</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Nested aggregator for the Java client (astubbs#242,
        docs/plans/2026-08-14-001-feat-language-proxy-plan.md): one shared wrapper surface, two
        transports behind it, and a lane that holds the sidecar dependency for both.

        THIS MODULE WAS A LEAF UNTIL THIS RUNG. It compiled one HelloFixture class asserting the
        polyglot scaffolding's fixture line, and its own pom said the rung bringing the real client
        would convert it and move that class out. That is this change: a Java module that compiles a
        client surface, two transports and their suites is a strictly stronger statement about the
        Java toolchain than a class that prints a string, so the fixture is deleted rather than
        relocated. The eight non-JVM modules keep theirs, and bin/foreign-client-step.sh still
        derives their expected line - nothing about that wrapper changes.

        The api module owns the user-facing surface; direct binds parallel-consumer-core in-process
        with no protobuf and no gRPC anywhere on its classpath (enforced in its pom); grpc speaks the
        frozen v1 wire to the sidecar; harness is a BUILD-GRAPH module rather than a product one,
        holding the sidecar dependency so no module a client wraps carries one - its pom explains
        what that buys and how to measure it.
    -->
    <modules>
        <module>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-api</module>
    </modules>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
                STATIC ANALYSIS FOR THE JAVA CLIENT, AND IT FAILS THE BUILD (astubbs#242; the policy
                is in docs/client-static-analysis.md).

                Declared once here so every module beneath it inherits it - including any added
                later, which is the point of putting it here rather than in each child. The root pom
                already pluginManages this plugin - version, effort=Max, threshold=Medium, the
                fb-contrib/findsecbugs/slf4j detector set - so nothing is repeated; what is added is
                the binding and the fact that it GATES.

                WHY BEFORE `test` AND NOT AT `verify`, which is the plugin's own default phase: the
                gating PR lane runs `bin/ci-unit-test.sh`, which stops at `test`. A verify-bound
                check would run only in the push-to-master full build - after review, on the wrong
                side of the merge - and would read as covered the whole time. Bound before `test`,
                every one of `test`, `package` and `verify` reaches it, and so does
                `bin/build.sh -pl :<module> -am` on a laptop.

                WHY process-test-classes AND NOT process-classes, WHICH IS WHERE THIS ARRIVED FROM
                astubbs/parallel-consumer#293. The root pom sets includeTests=true, so SpotBugs also
                reads target/test-classes - and at process-classes that directory has not been
                written yet. On a clean build the gate silently analysed main code only; on the next
                incremental build, with test classes left over, the SAME tree failed with two
                findings in a test. A gate whose verdict depends on what a previous run left on disk
                is not a gate, and the discovery order here - clean pass, then incremental fail on an
                unchanged tree - is exactly how one goes unnoticed. process-test-classes is the first
                phase after test-compile and is still before `test`, so nothing above changes and the
                analysis is the same set every time.

                WHY `check` AND NOT `spotbugs`: the `spotbugs` goal writes a report and exits 0.
                `check` reads that report and fails, which is the difference between analysis
                nobody reads and a gate. It runs the analysis itself (@Execute), so there is no
                second execution to keep in step.

                THIS IS A HARDER GATE THAN THE REPO-WIDE LANE, deliberately. maven.yml's
                `static: spotbugs` job is core-scoped, baseline-excluded and non-blocking - it
                reports what is NEW against master and never reddens. That design suits a codebase
                with long-standing findings (docs/inflight/static-spotbugs-latent-findings.md);
                these modules have none, and starting a new module at zero is the one moment a
                clean-slate gate is free.

                DISMISSING A FINDING: the shape that keeps the reason next to the code is
                @SuppressFBWarnings at the site, and it is unavailable in the api module - the
                annotation needs com.github.spotbugs:spotbugs-annotations on the classpath, and that
                module is dependency-free by design. So a dismissal goes in the module's own
                spotbugs-exclude.xml, named class by class and pattern by pattern with the reason
                written beside it. A module-wide or pattern-wide exclusion is the thing to refuse:
                it silences the finding for code nobody has written yet. Every entry in every module's
                filter names a class and a pattern, most of them a method or a field as well, and
                each carries the argument for declining it rather than a label.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>com.github.spotbugs</groupId>
                <artifactId>spotbugs-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- version and configuration come from the root pom's pluginManagement -->
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>spotbugs-gate</id>
                        <phase>process-test-classes</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>check</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
